# Sinfonia No. 6 in E major, BWV 792 (J. S. Bach)
#
# Character: a gentle 9/8 pastoral. The subject is a plain rising scale
# in eighths (E F# G# A B C# B C# A), set against long tied notes that
# make a chain of suspensions. Nothing in it should feel driven: it flows
# in three slow beats to the bar.
#
# Tempo: dotted quarter = 76. The bar count is 40.67 bars of 9/8, so 122
# dotted quarters. Leonhardt (harpsichord) takes 1:30, about 81 to the
# dotted quarter; Schiff (Decca) 1:17, about 95. Gould's 1964 track is
# listed at 0:52, far quicker than anyone else (not used). 76 sits just
# under Leonhardt: unhurried, so the plain chip tone does not sound rushed.
#
# Structure (bar numbers from the MIDI, which matches the score; the
# Mutopia source's bar comments drift and were not trusted):
#   1      subject in the alto, on E (bass holds E, D#)
#   2      subject in the soprano, on B
#   3-4    subject in the bass, on B
#   6-7    alto, then bass, on E again
#   9-10   alto, then soprano, heading for C# minor
#   11     cadence in C# minor; bars 11-15 episode of broken thirds
#          falling in the bass
#   16     subject in the alto (B major)
#   18     cadence in B major (the dominant), subject in the bass
#   19-22  alto, then a rising chain in the soprano (20-22)
#   23     arrival in C# minor; bars 23-29 the subject inverted
#          (falling scale): alto 23, soprano 24, bass 25, alto 26, bass 29
#   30-33  subject upright again, stacked one bar apart over a bass
#          rising B#, C#, F#, D#: alto 30, soprano 31, alto 32, soprano 33
#   34     fermata on the dominant seventh with A in the bass
#   35-37  resolution to E; entries in the alto (35) and bass (36)
#   38-41  soprano sixteenths, last entry 39, final cadence in E (41)

tempo 76 4.

# Dynamics, terraced and changing at cadences.
dyn 1 mp          # bars 1-10: opening exposition, quiet and even
dyn 11 p          # bars 11-17: after the C# minor cadence, the episode
                  # of broken thirds, a softer second manual
dyn 18 mf         # bars 18-22: after the B major cadence, the second
                  # half opens with the subject in the bass
dyn 23 mp         # bars 23-29: C# minor and the inverted subject, held back
ramp 30-33 mf     # bars 30-33: entries stack one bar apart and climb
                  # to the fermata at bar 34; this is the piece's one arc
broaden 34 0.7    # bar 34: the fermata on the dominant seventh, a real
                  # pause before the resolution to E at bar 35

# Subject entries brought forward. Voices are ranked by pitch per note,
# so when the soprano rests the alto counts as "top".
lead 1 top        # bar 1: alto has the subject, soprano silent
lead 2 top        # bar 2: soprano answers on B
lead 3 bottom     # bar 3: bass on B
lead 6 mid        # bar 6: alto on E
lead 7 bottom     # bar 7: bass on E
lead 9 mid        # bar 9: alto, towards C# minor
lead 10 top       # bar 10: soprano
lead 16 mid       # bar 16: alto, in B major
lead 18 bottom    # bar 18: bass, after the B major cadence
lead 19 top       # bar 19: alto, alone above the bass (soprano rests)
lead 20-22 top    # bars 20-22: soprano's rising chain
lead 25 bottom    # bar 25: bass, inverted subject
lead 29 bottom    # bar 29: bass, inverted subject
lead 31 top       # bar 31: soprano in the stacked entries
lead 33 top       # bar 33: soprano, last step before the fermata
lead 36 bottom    # bar 36: bass entry on A#, the chromatic turn

# Final cadence: the last bar and the short final bar ease to 0.7.
rit 2 0.7
